/// What a run is given to do: `jobs(for:in:urls:)`, which turns the clips a
/// caller named into `Job`s and says in `CaptionLayout.Problem`'s vocabulary
/// why it refused the rest, `contentLength(of:)`, the one place a clip's
/// source window is measured for them, and `hasTranscribableClip(in:)`, the
/// cheap question the menu's validator asks.
///
/// It arrived as a pure move out of `Transcriber.swift` (road map 6's C1
/// toll 1), and it is no longer one: c1-run added `maxSourceTicks`, the
/// bound on `Job.sourceIn`, the clamp inside `contentLength(of:)` and
/// `placed(offset:in:)`, the one door every `Double → Ticks` conversion on
/// the caption path goes through. One widening survives from the move:
/// `contentLength(of:)` is internal rather than `private` (road map 6 §5h
/// names it), which no compiler error demanded — its one caller, `jobs`,
/// moved with it.
///
/// **Why anything here is bounded at all — spec §11's R6-10.** A clip's
/// `speed` is bounded to `SPEED_MIN`/`SPEED_MAX` by the OPS
/// (`core/src/edit_clip_timing.rs`: `set_speed` clamps, `rate_stretch` is an
/// identity outside) and, since R6-10's amendment (#41), by the reader too
/// — `Project::validate`'s `check_clips` refuses a clip outside them — so
/// no opened project reaches this code with a tiny or huge speed. The
/// clamps here stay as the second layer R6-10 keeps, because if one ever
/// did, the two ends of the arithmetic would fail in two places: a huge
/// speed overflows the SOURCE LENGTH here, and a tiny one overflows the
/// OFFSET in the two mappings. Both clamp in `Double`, before the
/// conversion, because a `Ticks(_:)` on an out-of-range `Double` traps.
extension Transcriber {

    /// The ceiling on any source time this file answers: 1e15 ticks, about
    /// 1.9 billion seconds, some fifty years of media.
    ///
    /// Deliberately NOT `Ticks.max`, which is what the core's own
    /// `content_length_in_source` saturates at (`core/src/source_window.rs`
    /// casts an `f64` and an out-of-range cast saturates in Rust rather than
    /// trapping). The core never ADDS that number to anything; Swift does.
    /// `MediaTranscript.words` adds it to `sourceIn` for the window's end,
    /// and `TranscribeSheetController.refresh` sums it over every job, at
    /// most `MAX_CLIPS` = 4096 of them — and 4096 × 1e15 is under 2^63 with
    /// three orders of magnitude to spare, where 4096 × `Ticks.max` is not.
    static let maxSourceTicks: Ticks = 1_000_000_000_000_000

    /// One offset in ticks, as a `Double`, landed in the clip — the ONE
    /// door both caption mappings convert through.
    ///
    /// The clamp is into `[0, duration]` and it happens in `Double`: an
    /// offset divided by a speed of 1e-300 is a finite `Double` far larger
    /// than `Ticks.max`, and `Ticks(_:)` on it traps rather than saturating.
    /// Rounded half away from zero, the rule both mappings had. NaN — which
    /// is what `0 / 0` answers when a word sits exactly on a zero-speed
    /// clip's `in` point — is 0, the clip's own start: the beginning of the
    /// clip is the one answer that is always inside it.
    static func placed(offset: Double, in job: Job) -> Ticks {
        guard !offset.isNaN else { return job.start }
        let bounded = min(max(offset, 0), Double(job.duration))
        return job.start + Ticks(bounded.rounded(.toNearestOrAwayFromZero))
    }

    // MARK: - What can be transcribed

    /// Turns clips into jobs, and says why it refused the rest.
    ///
    /// Only a `media` clip whose file exists and has sound can be
    /// transcribed. A `still`, `image`, `text`, `shape` or `nest` source
    /// cannot — `edit.rs`'s non-media rule, in five names — and neither can a
    /// **reversed** clip, whose audio plays backwards and whose transcript
    /// would be nonsense presented as fact.
    ///
    /// "Has sound" is `InspectorTab.hasAudio`'s question and not the media's:
    /// the FILE having an audio track is only half of it, and `enabled.audio`
    /// is the other half (`audio.rs` gives such a clip no voice at all, so the
    /// timeline plays nothing for it). Detach Audio is what makes the
    /// difference visible — it leaves the picture clip with `enabled.audio`
    /// false and puts an audio-only twin with the SAME media, `in`, `start`
    /// and `speed` on the track below, so asking the media would recognise one
    /// recording twice, place two identical cues at one time, and then report
    /// the pile-up it made as captions that had to be shortened.
    ///
    /// The three-argument form is the instrument door old trees' probes
    /// build: it reads no map, so it refuses a clip whose speed ramps (spec
    /// §11 SR-20 (f)) rather than place its words at one rate.
    static func jobs(
        for clips: [ClipSnapshot], in snapshot: ProjectSnapshot, urls: [UInt32: URL]
    ) -> (jobs: [Job], problems: [CaptionLayout.Problem]) {
        jobs(for: clips, in: snapshot, urls: urls, maps: { _ in nil })
    }

    /// `jobs(for:in:urls:)` with the source maps of the handle `snapshot`
    /// came from (spec §11 SR-20 (d)): a clip whose map is RAMPED gets its
    /// window from the map — `sourceIn` its first source tick, `sourceLength`
    /// what it reads, held to `maxSourceTicks` — and carries the map in
    /// `Job.ramp`; `speed` stays the base. A constant map, or none, is the
    /// old job exactly. A clip whose actions name `end.speed` with no ramped
    /// map is refused, by SR-20 (f)'s sentence.
    static func jobs(
        for clips: [ClipSnapshot], in snapshot: ProjectSnapshot, urls: [UInt32: URL],
        maps: SourceMaps
    ) -> (jobs: [Job], problems: [CaptionLayout.Problem]) {
        var jobs: [Job] = []
        var problems: [CaptionLayout.Problem] = []
        for clip in clips.sorted(by: { $0.start < $1.start }) {
            let tag = "clip \(clip.id)"
            guard case .media(let media, let sourceIn, let reversed) = clip.source else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(
                        tag: tag, reason: "its source is not a media file, so it has no speech"))
                continue
            }
            guard !reversed else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(
                        tag: tag,
                        reason: "it plays in reverse, so its speech cannot be transcribed"))
                continue
            }
            guard let entry = snapshot.media(media), entry.hasAudio else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(tag: tag, reason: "its media has no audio track"))
                continue
            }
            guard clip.enabled.audio else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(tag: tag, reason: "its audio is switched off"))
                continue
            }
            guard let url = urls[media] else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(tag: tag, reason: "its media file is missing"))
                continue
            }
            guard clip.speed.isFinite, clip.speed > 0, clip.duration > 0 else {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(tag: tag, reason: "its speed or length is degenerate"))
                continue
            }
            let map = maps(clip.id).flatMap { $0.ramped ? $0 : nil }
            if map == nil, clip.actions.contains(where: { $0.end.speed != nil }) {
                problems.append(
                    CaptionLayout.Problem(
                        tag: tag, reason: "its speed ramps, and no speed map was read for it"))
                continue
            }
            if let map = map {
                jobs.append(
                    Job(
                        clip: clip.id, media: media, name: snapshot.name(of: clip), source: url,
                        sourceIn: min(max(0, map.sourceIn), maxSourceTicks),
                        sourceLength: min(map.sourceLength, maxSourceTicks),
                        start: clip.start, duration: clip.duration, speed: clip.speed,
                        ramp: Job.Ramp(map: map, clipStart: clip.start)))
                continue
            }
            jobs.append(
                Job(
                    clip: clip.id, media: media, name: snapshot.name(of: clip), source: url,
                    sourceIn: min(max(0, sourceIn), maxSourceTicks),
                    sourceLength: contentLength(of: clip),
                    start: clip.start, duration: clip.duration, speed: clip.speed))
        }
        return (jobs, problems)
    }

    /// How much SOURCE time a clip plays — `Project::content_length_in_source`
    /// (`core/src/source_window.rs`) in Swift, because the extract's range is
    /// a source range and the core owns what that means.
    ///
    /// It does NOT saturate where the core saturates. The core's cast tops
    /// out at `i64::MAX`; this one tops out at `maxSourceTicks`, for the
    /// reason that constant's own doc gives — Swift sums this answer and the
    /// core does not. Every speed the ops can write (`SPEED_MIN`/`SPEED_MAX`)
    /// is orders of magnitude below the bound and answers exactly what it
    /// always did; R6-10 keeps it for speeds the reader now refuses (#41). The
    /// clamp is applied in `Double`: `Ticks(_:)` traps on an out-of-range
    /// one. The degenerate fallback is unchanged and is bounded too, since a
    /// hand-written `duration` can be as large as `Ticks` allows.
    ///
    /// A product that overflows to `+infinity` — which `duration × 1e308`
    /// does — is the bound and not the fallback: the honest answer to "how
    /// much source does this play" is "more than there can be", which is
    /// `maxSourceTicks`, where the clip's own duration would be a number
    /// pulled out of the air. `NaN`, a negative and `-infinity` all fail the
    /// `>= 1` test and take the fallback, as they always did.
    static func contentLength(of clip: ClipSnapshot) -> Ticks {
        let scaled = Double(clip.duration) * clip.speed
        guard scaled >= 1 else { return min(max(clip.duration, 1), maxSourceTicks) }
        return Ticks(min(scaled, Double(maxSourceTicks)).rounded())
    }

    /// Whether anything in this project could be transcribed at all — the
    /// menu item's validator, deliberately the cheap snapshot question rather
    /// than a plan, because a validator runs on every menu open.
    ///
    /// It asks the same three things `jobs` asks and in the same order, minus
    /// the two a snapshot cannot answer without the document's media URLs, so
    /// the item can only be enabled where a run would find work. `enabled.audio`
    /// is one of the three: a timeline whose every audible clip has had its
    /// sound switched off has nothing to transcribe, and a menu item that
    /// opens a sheet whose only answer is "nothing to transcribe" is the
    /// dialog `generateCaptionsCommand`'s guard exists to prevent.
    static func hasTranscribableClip(in snapshot: ProjectSnapshot) -> Bool {
        snapshot.clips.contains { clip in
            guard case .media(let media, _, let reversed) = clip.source, !reversed,
                  clip.enabled.audio
            else {
                return false
            }
            return snapshot.media(media)?.hasAudio ?? false
        }
    }
}

extension Transcriber.Job {
    /// The job's clip on the timeline, `[start, start + duration)` — the ONE
    /// spelling of it. `CaptionPages` confines every page it makes for this
    /// job to it (road map 8's W4a), so pages of two abutting clips abut at
    /// most. `duration` is positive: `jobs(for:in:urls:)` refuses a clip
    /// whose length is not.
    var span: Range<Ticks> { start ..< start + duration }
}

    }

    /// Reads a document's snapshot, plan, selection and peak caches.
    ///
    /// `selection` overrides the document's own, which is what the agent's
    /// `detect_silence {clips}` argument needs; the sheet passes nil.
    /// nil is answered only when the core emitted a plan this build cannot
    /// read — the same failure `CompositionBuilder` treats as fatal.
    static func read(from document: ProjectDocument, selection: Set<UInt32>? = nil) -> Input? {
        // The EDITED project (road map 2's phase 9): with a nest open the
        // scope, the plan and the ranges `ripple_delete_ranges` is sent are
        // all the NEST's, in the nest's own time, and the op carries the
        // scope. At the top level this is `document.project` as before.
        guard let plan = document.editedProject.plan else { return nil }
        let snapshot = document.editedProject.snapshot
        let cacheDirectory = document.cacheDirectory

        // One read per media, however many segments cite it — `MediaPeaks.loader`
        // (a `MediaCacheLoader`) is that memo, and its `pending` set is what
        // this reports as "still analysing".
        let loader = MediaPeaks.loader(cacheDirectory: cacheDirectory)
        // One read per media, exactly `loader`'s shape, for the voice cache.
        let speechLoader = MediaSpeech.loader(cacheDirectory: cacheDirectory)

        var voices: [Voice] = []
        for track in plan.audioTracks {
            for segment in track.segments where segment.duration > 0 {
                let span = (segment.start, segment.start + segment.duration)
                voices.append(
                    Voice(
                        segment: segment, ramps: track.ramps,
                        peaks: loader.value(for: segment.media),
                        reversedPivot: MediaPeaks.reversedPivot(for: segment, in: snapshot),
                        audible: isAudible(track.ramps, over: span),
                        speech: speechLoader.value(for: segment.media)))
            }
        }
        let nests = nestExtents(in: snapshot)
        return Input(
            snapshot: snapshot, selection: selection ?? document.selection,
            pendingMedia: loader.pending.sorted(), voiceAvailable: SileroVAD.isAvailable,
            pendingSpeech: speechLoader.pending.sorted(), voices: voices, nests: nests,
            walls: overlapWalls(snapshot))
    }

    // MARK: - Detection

    /// The quiet spans, in project ticks, snapped to the frame grid.
    ///
    /// The walk is over PROJECT time in bucket-sized steps, so the resolution
    /// is one bucket (10 ms) however fast or slow a clip is playing. Within
    /// one step a segment's loudness is the MAX over every bucket the step
    /// covers, which is the conservative reading for a destructive edit: a
    /// clip at ×2 covers two buckets per step and taking the louder of them
    /// can only keep material, never cut more.
    ///
    /// A tick covered by NO segment in scope is not a candidate. "Remove
    /// Silences" means "take the dead air out of my recording", not "close
    /// every gap in my edit" — a hole between a title card and the footage is
    /// deliberate spacing and shading it would promise a cut nobody asked for.
    static func detect(_ input: Input, settings: Settings) -> Outcome {
        // `audible` is asked of BOTH scopes, not only of its namesake. A
        // voice the mix has silenced — muted, silenced by another track's
        // solo, turned down, faded to nothing — reads as quiet at every tick
        // by construction, because `level` short-circuits on a zero envelope.
        // In scope it would therefore COVER its whole span while never
        // failing the quiet test, and a selected muted music bed would
        // propose one cut across the entire song, taking the audible
        // voiceover under it with the ripple. Dropping it keeps the reading
        // this file is built on — a silenced clip does not BLOCK a cut — and
        // leaves a span nothing audible covers a hole, which is never a
        // candidate.
        let scoped = input.voices.filter { voice in
            guard voice.audible else { return false }
            switch settings.scope {
            case .selected: return input.selection.contains(voice.segment.clip)
            case .audible: return true
            }
        }
        let clips = Set(scoped.map { $0.segment.clip }).sorted()
        let pending = Set(scoped.filter { $0.peaks == nil }.map { $0.segment.media }).sorted()
        guard let speech = voice.speech else { return false }
        return Double(
            MediaSpeech.speechMax(
                speech, over: voice.segment, from: from, to: to,
                reversedPivot: voice.reversedPivot)) < voiceConfidence
    }

    /// Whether a track's envelope is anything but zero across a span. The
    /// ramps cover a track's whole extent with no gaps, so a span nothing
    /// covers is a plan this build does not understand — believe the segment
    /// rather than silence it.
    private static func isAudible(_ ramps: [PlanRamp]?, over span: (Ticks, Ticks)) -> Bool {
        guard let ramps = ramps, !ramps.isEmpty else { return true }
        var covered = false
        for ramp in ramps {
            let end = ramp.start + max(0, ramp.duration)
            guard end > span.0, ramp.start < span.1 else { continue }
            covered = true
            if max(ramp.from, ramp.to) > 0 { return true }
        }
        return !covered
    }

}

//! Flattening nests: the walk that turns one nest clip into the clips it
//! stands for (spec §5.3, §7.2, §7.3).
//!
//! A [`Nest`] is a group of tracks and clips held in the parent's ONE id
//! space, referencing the parent's `media[]`, with its times measured from
//! its own start. Nothing downstream of this module has to know that. The
//! plan, the recipe and the envelope all ask the same question — *which
//! clips actually play on this timeline track, and when* — and get back a
//! flat list of [`Instance`]s whose times are already project times and
//! whose extents are already clipped to the nest clip that shows them. A
//! project with no nests produces exactly one instance per clip, so the
//! flattened path IS the ordinary path and there is no second code route to
//! keep in step.
//!
//! Its own module because the walk is a new concern and `evaluate.rs` is
//! over the size cap `core/CLAUDE.md` sets. It holds no policy about what a
//! layer looks like or how loud it is: it decides only what exists, where,
//! and inside what.
//!
//! ### Four decisions the walk makes
//!
//! 1. **A nest clip has no in point.** `Source::Nest` carries a nest id and
//!    nothing else (spec §5.2), so a nest always plays from its own first
//!    frame. Trimming the head of a nest clip therefore MOVES its contents
//!    later rather than cutting into them, and splitting one leaves two
//!    clips that each replay the nest from the top. That is the honest
//!    consequence of the schema; the alternative — a fourth source field —
//!    is not version 2's.
//! 2. **Contents are clipped, never stretched.** A nest clip shorter than
//!    its nest cuts the tail off (and a clip that falls entirely outside it
//!    is dropped); a longer one draws nothing over the surplus. The nest
//!    clip's duration is independent of `Nest::duration` for exactly this
//!    reason.
//! 3. **Ids are not re-minted.** A nest lives in the parent's one id space
//!    (see [`Nest`]), which is what lets a segment name a media id the app
//!    can resolve and a `clip_state` call name a nested clip by one
//!    integer. But two clips may show the SAME nest, so one clip id can
//!    appear at two places on the timeline: an instance is named by its
//!    [`Instance::path`] — the enclosing nest clip ids, outermost first,
//!    then its own — which is unique, ordered, and what the composition
//!    plan keys its clip → video track map by.
//! 4. **The order is the stacking order.** Instances come out in the order
//!    they are drawn: timeline track by timeline track, clips in start
//!    order, and a nest clip replaced in place by its own stack walked the
//!    same way. So a nest's layers occupy exactly the slot the nest clip
//!    occupied, back to front, which is what makes a nest look the same
//!    after it is made as it did before.

